consiSt("md",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Maryland","d":"Seven justices, one from each of seven Appellate Judicial Circuits; the Governor designates one as Chief Justice. Five justices sit in each case unless the Court directs more. A 3–3 or 3–2 division entitles the losing party, on application, to reargument before all seven as of right. Reviews cases mostly by certiorari, answers certified questions of law, and adopts the Maryland Rules. Formerly the Court of Appeals; renamed December 14, 2022 (date per the request; the statute text collected uses only the new names)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Appellate Court of Maryland","d":"Has exclusive initial appellate jurisdiction over reviewable judgments, orders and other actions of the circuit courts and orphans’ courts, except where the Supreme Court has jurisdiction. Formerly the Court of Special Appeals."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Courts (one per county and Baltimore City)","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts. They hear jury trials, equity and declaratory-judgment cases, and appeals from the District Court and agencies. Civil procedure is governed by Title 2 of the Maryland Rules."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"District Court of Maryland","d":"Statewide court of limited jurisdiction. It has exclusive civil jurisdiction over contract and tort claims up to $30,000, landlord-tenant and replevin cases regardless of amount, and small claims up to $5,000. It has no general equity jurisdiction and cannot issue declaratory judgments. Above $5,000 the plaintiff may choose the District Court or the circuit court. Civil procedure is governed by Title 3 of the Maryland Rules."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: Courts & Judicial Proceedings § 5-807","d":"A suit is a SLAPP suit only if all three elements are met: (1) it is brought in bad faith against a party who communicated with a federal, State or local government body or the public at large on a matter within a government body’s authority or an issue of public concern, exercising rights under the First Amendment or Articles 10, 13 or 40 of the Maryland Declaration of Rights; (2) it is materially related to that communication; and (3) it is intended to inhibit, or does inhibit, those rights.\nA SLAPP defendant who acted without constitutional malice is not civilly liable for the communication.","c":"Md. Code, Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 5-807(a)-(c)","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: motion to dismiss or stay; no stated deadline","d":"The defendant may move to (1) dismiss, and the court must hold a hearing ‘as soon as practicable’; or (2) stay all court proceedings until the underlying matter the defendant communicated about is resolved.\nThe statute sets no filing deadline, no discovery stay, no evidentiary burden-shifting, and no time limit for the ruling. It applies notwithstanding any other law or rule, and it preserves every other legal or equitable remedy.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 5-807(d)-(e)","v":true},{"t":"No fee-shifting and no special appeal right","d":"Section 5-807 contains no attorney’s fee or cost award. It does not create an immediate appeal from denial of the motion, and denial of a § 5-807 motion is not among the interlocutory orders listed as appealable in CJ § 12-303. Whether any other route to early review exists (for example, the collateral order doctrine) was not checked; the case law was not collected.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 5-807, 12-303","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: Rule 19-217 (rule text not collected)","d":"Out-of-state lawyers appear in a particular Maryland case by special admission (pro hac vice) under Maryland Rule 19-217. That rule could not be read because mdcourts.gov blocked automated access, so its requirements are not stated here: the motion contents, the Maryland sponsoring lawyer, any limit on the number of admissions, and any fee. Read the current rule before filing.","c":"Md. Rule 19-217 (not verified)","v":false},{"t":"Statute: admission required to practice, and the special-admission statute for employed lawyers","d":"An individual must be admitted to the Maryland Bar, and meet any requirement the Supreme Court sets by rule, before practicing law in Maryland. Unauthorized practice is prohibited.\nSeparately, a court may grant special admission for one case to a lawyer admitted elsewhere who is employed by a party in a case before a court or a State or local unit. The motion must be filed as the Supreme Court’s rules require. The court hearing the case grants it; for a case before an agency, it is the circuit court for the county of the agency’s principal office or any circuit court the case may be appealed to. The admission covers only that case, and the lawyer is subject to Maryland discipline.","c":"Bus. Occ. & Prof. §§ 10-206(a), 10-215, 10-601(a)","v":true},{"t":"In-house counsel admitted elsewhere may advise the employer without Maryland admission","d":"A lawyer admitted in another state may give legal advice to the employer and the employer’s affiliates without Maryland admission. That lawyer is subject to Maryland discipline and may not appear before a State or local government unit without a special admission under § 10-215. Any registration requirement under the Maryland Rules was not checked.","c":"Bus. Occ. & Prof. § 10-206(d)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation and e-filing (MDEC): not verified","d":"The Maryland Rules on limited-scope appearances and on electronic filing through MDEC (Maryland Electronic Courts; e-filing rules are reported to be in Title 20) could not be collected. Coverage, mandatory-filer status, and registration steps for out-of-state counsel are not stated here. Check Title 20 and the MDEC pages on mdcourts.gov.","c":"Md. Rules Title 20 (not verified)","v":false}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Maryland Attorneys’ Rules of Professional Conduct (not collected)","d":"Maryland lawyers are governed by the Maryland Attorneys’ Rules of Professional Conduct, Rules 19-300.1 ff., in Title 19 of the Maryland Rules. They follow the ABA Model Rules numbering after the ‘19-3’ prefix (for example, 19-301.6 is confidentiality). The text could not be collected from mdcourts.gov, so no differences from the ABA Model Rules are stated here.","c":"Md. Rules 19-300.1 ff. (not verified)","v":false},{"t":"Supreme Court’s rule power and discipline of out-of-state lawyers (statute)","d":"The Supreme Court of Maryland adopts rules on practice, procedure and court administration, and those rules have the force of law. Out-of-state lawyers who advise their employers or practice under special admission are expressly subject to disciplinary proceedings under the Maryland Rules.","c":"Md. Const. art. IV, § 18(a); Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 1-201(a); Bus. Occ. & Prof. §§ 10-206(d)(3)(i), 10-215(d)","v":true}],"style":"State of Maryland","home":"https://www.mdcourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"The Maryland Rules, not the Federal Rules","d":"Circuit court civil practice is under Title 2 of the Maryland Rules, District Court practice under Title 3, and appeals under Title 8. Their numbering and deadlines differ from the federal rules. These rules have the force of law. (Rule text was not collected for this page; read the current Rules.)","c":"Md. Const. art. IV, § 18(a); Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 1-201"},{"t":"Two different trial courts, and the $5,000 / $30,000 lines","d":"The District Court has exclusive jurisdiction over contract and tort claims up to $30,000 and over small claims up to $5,000. Above $5,000 (except replevin, landlord-tenant and certain other cases) the plaintiff may choose the District Court or the circuit court. Claims for equity relief (outside the listed exceptions), title to real property, and declaratory judgments must go to the circuit court. The $30,000 cap changes under laws effective June 30, 2028 and 2029; check the version in effect.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 4-401(1), 4-402(a)-(d), 4-405"},{"t":"District Court appeal: 30 days, filed in the District Court, often de novo","d":"Appeals from District Court civil judgments go to the circuit court for the same county (or Baltimore City), not to the Appellate Court. File the order for appeal with the District Court clerk within 30 days of the final judgment; certain landlord-tenant cases have shorter, section-specific times. If more than $5,000 is in controversy (excluding interest, costs and recoverable fees), or the parties agree, the appeal is on the record. Otherwise it is tried de novo.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-401(a), (e), (f), 12-403"},{"t":"No further appeal of right after the circuit court reviews the District Court","d":"A circuit court judgment entered in its appellate capacity (reviewing the District Court, an agency or a local legislative body) cannot be appealed under § 12-301 unless a statute expressly grants the right. The only route is a petition for certiorari to the Supreme Court of Maryland, granted for uniformity of decision or special public-interest circumstances.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-302(a), 12-305"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeals are a closed statutory list","d":"Civil interlocutory appeals lie only from the orders listed in § 12-303. Examples: possession of property; injunctions (an appeal from granting an injunction, refusing to dissolve one, or appointing a receiver requires the appellant to have filed an answer first); orders for payment of money or sale of property; certain custody orders; and orders staying arbitration. Denial of an anti-SLAPP motion is not on the list.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-303(3)(i), (ii), (iv), (v), (ix), (x)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP is weak: bad faith required, no fees, no deadline","d":"Unlike many states’ statutes, § 5-807 requires the plaintiff to have sued in bad faith and with intent to inhibit, or with the effect of inhibiting, protected rights. It provides only a hearing ‘as soon as practicable’ or a stay. There is no fee award and no automatic discovery stay.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 5-807(b), (d)"},{"t":"Venue: negligence torts may be filed where the claim arose; nonresident defendants anywhere","d":"General venue is where a defendant resides, carries on regular business, is employed or habitually works (and for a corporation, also its principal Maryland office). A negligence tort may also be brought where it arose. A damages action against a nonresident individual may be brought in any county. With several defendants and no common venue, sue where any defendant could be sued or where the claim arose.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 6-201, 6-202(8), (11)"},{"t":"Three-year default limitations period","d":"A civil action at law must be filed within three years from accrual unless another Code provision sets a different period.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 5-101"},{"t":"Supreme Court review is by certiorari, and some leave denials are final","d":"Review of Appellate Court cases is by petition for certiorari, which may be filed before or after the Appellate Court decides, within the time set by the Maryland Rules. The writ issues if review is ‘desirable and in the public interest.’ Certiorari is barred where the Appellate Court granted or denied leave to appeal in categories listed in § 12-202, such as guilty-plea judgments and probation revocations.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-201, 12-202, 12-203"},{"t":"Pro hac vice and professional conduct rules are not on this page","d":"Rule 19-217 (pro hac vice) and the Maryland Attorneys’ Rules of Professional Conduct could not be collected (mdcourts.gov bot block). Read them directly before appearing; do not rely on another state’s practice for the sponsor, fee, or numerical limits.","c":"Md. Rules 19-217, 19-300.1 ff. (not verified)"}],"judge":[{"t":"Constitutional removal of a case to another county","d":"Maryland has no peremptory challenge to a judge in the statute text collected. Instead, the Constitution lets a party remove a case to another court. In a capital case, a sworn written suggestion that the party cannot get a fair and impartial trial is enough. In other criminal cases, in suits at law, and in issues from an orphans’ court, the party must also show the court that the suggestion is true or that there is reasonable ground for it. Removal is also available when every judge of the court is disqualified.\nThe procedural rule implementing removal in civil cases (reportedly Rule 2-505) and its deadlines could not be checked.","c":"Md. Const. art. IV, § 8(b)-(c); Md. Rule 2-505 (not verified)","v":true},{"t":"Recusal: governed by the Code of Judicial Conduct (not collected)","d":"Disqualification standards are in the Maryland Code of Judicial Conduct in Title 18 of the Maryland Rules, which could not be collected. One statutory ground is verified: while a judge is receiving buyout payments from a former law firm, the judge may not hear a case in which a partner or employee of that firm, or its successor, is attorney of record. If a justice or judge is disqualified, the Chief Justice may assign another judge to sit.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 1-203(b)-(c); Md. Const. art. IV, § 18(b)(2); Md. Rules Title 18 (not verified)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Declaration of Rights Articles 10, 13 and 40","d":"Article 40 protects the liberty of the press and every citizen’s right to speak, write and publish on all subjects, ‘being responsible for the abuse of that privilege.’ Article 13 protects petitioning the Legislature. Article 10 protects legislative speech and debate. The anti-SLAPP statute expressly protects activity under all three. Whether Maryland courts read Article 40 more broadly than the First Amendment was not established from collected materials.","c":"Md. Decl. of Rights arts. 10, 13, 40; Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 5-807(b)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"2022 court renaming","d":"The Court of Appeals is now the Supreme Court of Maryland, and the Court of Special Appeals is now the Appellate Court of Maryland. The current statutes and Constitution use only the new names. Older cases, rules and forms use the old names; cite the current names.","c":"Md. Const. art. IV, § 14; Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-307, 12-308","v":true},{"t":"District Court jury demand moves the case","d":"In the District Court no jury trial may be demanded if the amount in controversy is $25,000 or less, excluding attorney’s fees. If a party entitled to a jury files a timely demand, jurisdiction transfers at once to the circuit court (replevin has special handling).","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 4-402(e)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Md. Code, Courts and Judicial Proceedings §§ 1-201, 4-402, 12-201 to 12-203, 12-301 to 12-305, 12-307, 12-308, 12-401, 12-403; Md. Const. art. IV, §§ 14, 18, from mgaleg.maryland.gov (current law, fetched November 10, 2026). Title 8 of the Maryland Rules (appellate procedure) was NOT collected: mdcourts.gov blocked automated access with a captcha, and the Rules are not on mgaleg.maryland.gov. Every Rule-based point below is marked not verified.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Maryland: three routes, set by which court decided the case","d":"From a circuit court final judgment, appeal goes to the Appellate Court of Maryland (formerly the Court of Special Appeals). From the District Court, a civil appeal goes to the circuit court for the same county, on the record or de novo depending on the amount. Beyond that, review is only by certiorari in the Supreme Court of Maryland (formerly the Court of Appeals).\nThe statutes set who may appeal and from what. The Maryland Rules (Title 8) set most deadlines, briefing and word limits, and could not be collected for this page."},"groups":[["What can be appealed",[{"t":"Final judgments of circuit courts","d":"A party may appeal from a final judgment of a circuit court in a civil or criminal case. This includes judgments in special, limited or statutory jurisdiction unless a law expressly denies the appeal. The Appellate Court has exclusive initial appellate jurisdiction.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-301, 12-308"},{"t":"No appeal of right from a circuit court acting as an appellate court","d":"When a circuit court reviews the District Court, an administrative agency or a local legislative body, its judgment is not appealable under § 12-301 unless a statute expressly grants an appeal. Seek certiorari in the Supreme Court instead (see below).","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-302(a)"},{"t":"Interlocutory orders: only those listed","d":"Civil interlocutory orders are appealable only if listed in § 12-303. The list includes: orders about possession of the property in suit or its income; orders granting or denying a motion to quash an attachment; orders granting, dissolving, refusing to dissolve, or refusing an injunction; orders appointing a receiver; orders for sale or delivery of property or payment of money; orders determining a question of right and directing an account; orders staying arbitration; custody orders depriving a parent, grandparent or guardian of care and custody; denials of immunity under §§ 5-525 or 5-526; and certain limitations or repose rulings on § 5-117 claims.\nAppeals from an order granting an injunction, refusing to dissolve one, or appointing a receiver require that the appellant has first filed an answer.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-303"},{"t":"Contempt","d":"Any person adjudged in contempt may appeal, including from an interlocutory remedial contempt order and whether or not a party. This does not apply to contempt for violating an interlocutory alimony order.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-304"},{"t":"Cross-appeal after remittitur","d":"In a civil case, a plaintiff who accepted a remittitur may still cross-appeal from the final judgment. The general timing and procedure for cross-appeals are in Title 8 of the Rules (not verified).","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-301"}]],["Timing and tolling",[{"t":"Notice of appeal from circuit court: Rule 8-202 (not verified)","d":"The time to note an appeal from a circuit court judgment, and the post-judgment motions that extend it, are set by Maryland Rule 8-202. The rule text was not collected. It is commonly cited as 30 days after entry of judgment, but confirm the period, the tolling motions (for example, motions under Rules 2-532, 2-533 and 2-534) and their own deadlines in the current rule.","c":"Md. Rule 8-202 (not verified)"},{"t":"District Court civil appeal: 30 days, filed with the District Court clerk","d":"File an order for appeal with the District Court clerk within 30 days from the date of the final judgment. Judgments in landlord-tenant and related cases under Real Property §§ 8-332, 8-401, 8-402, 14-109 and 14-120 follow the time set in those sections.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-401(e)"}]],["District Court appeals",[{"t":"Where and how they are heard","d":"The appeal goes to the circuit court for the county where judgment was entered, or the Circuit Court for Baltimore City. It is heard on the District Court record if the amount in controversy exceeds $5,000 (excluding interest, costs and recoverable fees), in matters under § 4-401(7)(ii), or when the parties agree. Every other appeal is tried de novo.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-401(f), 12-403"}]],["Briefs, word limits and e-filing",[{"t":"Briefing schedule and limits (not verified)","d":"Briefing deadlines, record extract requirements, and word or page limits for the Appellate Court and the Supreme Court are in Title 8 of the Maryland Rules, which could not be collected. No numbers are given here; read Title 8 (including Rules 8-501 to 8-504 on the record extract and briefs, as commonly cited) before filing.","c":"Md. Rules Title 8 (not verified)"},{"t":"Electronic filing (MDEC) (not verified)","d":"Appellate e-filing through MDEC is governed by Title 20 of the Maryland Rules, which could not be collected. Check Title 20 and the Appellate Court’s current instructions.","c":"Md. Rules Title 20 (not verified)"}]],["Supreme Court of Maryland",[{"t":"Certiorari from the Appellate Court, before or after decision","d":"Any party may petition for certiorari in a case pending in or decided by the Appellate Court on appeal from a circuit court, an orphans’ court or the Tax Court. The petition may be filed before or after the Appellate Court decides, within the time set by the Maryland Rules (not verified). The Supreme Court may also grant certiorari on its own motion. It grants the writ when review is ‘desirable and in the public interest.’ It may set by rule how many justices must vote to grant, but not more than three. Reasons for denial must be in writing.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. §§ 12-201, 12-203"},{"t":"Certiorari barred in some leave-to-appeal cases","d":"No certiorari lies where the Appellate Court granted or denied leave to appeal in: post-conviction cases; habeas corpus on bail; incarcerated individual grievance cases; guilty-plea judgments; and probation revocations.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-202"},{"t":"Certiorari to review a circuit court’s appellate judgment","d":"When a circuit court has decided an appeal from the District Court (or certain Transportation Article agency appeals), the Supreme Court will grant certiorari on petition only if review is needed for uniformity of decision, or other special circumstances make review desirable and in the public interest.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-305"},{"t":"Certified questions","d":"The Supreme Court has exclusive appellate jurisdiction over questions of law certified to it under the Uniform Certification of Questions of Law Act.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-307(3)"},{"t":"Reargument of right after a split decision","d":"Five justices normally sit. If the Court divides equally, or 3–2, the losing party is entitled on application to reargument before all seven justices as of right. An equal division with no application affirms the decision below. Motions for reconsideration in the Appellate Court and their deadlines are in Title 8 (not verified).","c":"Md. Const. art. IV, § 14"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal from a circuit court final judgment","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment or order appealed from.\nNOT VERIFIED: rule text not collected (mdcourts.gov blocked). The 30-day period is the commonly cited rule; confirm it and the tolling effect of timely post-judgment motions in the current Rule 8-202.","c":"Md. Rule 8-202(a) (not verified)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order appealed from)","note":"NOT VERIFIED: rule text not collected (mdcourts.gov blocked). The 30-day period is the commonly cited rule; confirm it and the tolling effect of timely post-judgment motions in the current Rule 8-202."}},{"t":"Civil appeal from the District Court to the circuit court","d":"30 days from date of the District Court final judgment.\nFile the order for appeal with the District Court clerk. Certain landlord-tenant judgments (Real Prop. §§ 8-332, 8-401, 8-402, 14-109, 14-120) have their own times.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-401(e)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date of the District Court final judgment)","note":"File the order for appeal with the District Court clerk. Certain landlord-tenant judgments (Real Prop. §§ 8-332, 8-401, 8-402, 14-109, 14-120) have their own times."}},{"t":"State’s pretrial appeal from suppression or return-of-property ruling (criminal)","d":"15 days from date the trial court decision is rendered.\nOnly in the case types listed in § 12-302(c)(4)(i), and before jeopardy attaches. The appellate decision is due within 120 days after the record is filed, or the trial ruling becomes final.","c":"Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-302(c)(4)(iii)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (date the trial court decision is rendered)","note":"Only in the case types listed in § 12-302(c)(4)(i), and before jeopardy attaches. The appellate decision is due within 120 days after the record is filed, or the trial ruling becomes final."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal from a circuit court final judgment",30,"Entry of the judgment or order appealed from","Md. Rule 8-202(a) (not verified)","NOT VERIFIED: rule text not collected (mdcourts.gov blocked). The 30-day period is the commonly cited rule; confirm it and the tolling effect of timely post-judgment motions in the current Rule 8-202."],["Civil appeal from the District Court to the circuit court",30,"Date of the District Court final judgment","Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-401(e)(1)","File the order for appeal with the District Court clerk. Certain landlord-tenant judgments (Real Prop. §§ 8-332, 8-401, 8-402, 14-109, 14-120) have their own times."],["State’s pretrial appeal from suppression or return-of-property ruling (criminal)",15,"Date the trial court decision is rendered","Cts. & Jud. Proc. § 12-302(c)(4)(iii)","Only in the case types listed in § 12-302(c)(4)(i), and before jeopardy attaches. The appellate decision is due within 120 days after the record is filed, or the trial ruling becomes final."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.mdcourts.gov/"]]}});
